From: j7
Subject: j7: drive the gauge the way the verified PMIC path does

Every high-speed bus still logged nothing, and the last three logged the same
`ts=0x2000c` - bit 17 of I2C_TRANS_STATUS, a stop phase that never finished.  That value
was inherited: this code never waited for a stop and never reset the block, so one
failed message left every bus that followed in the same state.

The same controller IP is device verified on this SoC generation in the A5XELTE PMIC
path, which reads the S2MPU03 through it.  Do what that code does: reset the block with
I2C_CTL bit 31, program Linux's timing registers, put the byte count in the FIFO
trigger the controller expects, service the transmit and receive FIFOs while the
transfer runs instead of around it, poll INT_STATUS and clear each bit as it arrives,
and do not report success until the stop phase has cleared.  A failed message now
resets the block, so the next bus starts clean.

The scan also asks 0x36, the MAX77854 gauge address the Exynos 7870 boards use next to
the PMIC at 0x66, instead of 0x0d, which is not a fuel gauge on any Samsung board.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

